## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of basic data structures beyond arrays, focusing on a Trie with counters to support prefix queries and duplicates, a common interview topic.

## Key Concepts

- Trie with per-node counters
- Path updates for insert/remove
- Prefix query via traverse returning passCount
- Edge cases: removing non-existent words and zero counts

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor(){ this.next=new Map(); this.pass=0; this.end=0; } }
class Trie {
  constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
  addWord(w){ let node=this.root; node.pass++; for(const ch of w){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node=node.next.get(ch); node.pass++; } node.end++; }
  removeWord(w){ let stack=[]; let node=this.root; stack.push(node); for(const ch of w){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return; node=node.next.get(ch); stack.push(node); } if(stack[stack.length-1].end===0) return; stack.forEach(n=>n.pass--); stack[stack.length-1].end--; }
  countPrefix(p){ let node=this.root; for(const ch of p){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node=node.next.get(ch); } return node.pass; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle memory usage for large alphabets or dynamic languages?
- How would you extend to support deletion of a specific instance when duplicates exist and maintain performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical data structure design for a common gaming feature, balancing efficient updates with query performance.

## Key Concepts
- Hash maps provide O(1) player score lookups
- Ordered maps enable range and top-k access
- Ordered sets handle duplicate scores within groups
- Rank computation accounts for tied scores

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode illustrating approach
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(){
    this.playerToScore = new Map();
    this.scoreToPlayers = new Map(); // descending keys; each maps to a Set of players
  }
  addOrUpdate(playerId, score) {
    // Remove from old score group if exists
    const oldScore = this.playerToScore.get(playerId);
    if (oldScore !== undefined) {
      const oldSet = this.scoreToPlayers.get(oldScore);
      oldSet.delete(playerId);
      if (oldSet.size === 0) this.scoreToPlayers.delete(oldScore);
    }
    // Update maps and add to new score group
    this.playerToScore.set(playerId, score);
    if (!this.scoreToPlayers.has(score)) {
      this.scoreToPlayers.set(score, new Set());
    }
    this.scoreToPlayers.get(score).add(playerId);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical DS reasoning for streams where both max and total are needed efficiently, using a familiar two-stack queue pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Two-stack queue with per-element max
- Running sum maintenance for O(1) sum queries
- Amortized analysis of transfer between stacks

## Code Example
```javascript
class MaxQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.in = [];
    this.out = [];
    this.total = 0;
  }
  push(x) {
    const newMax = this.in.length === 0 ? x : Math.max(x, this.in[this.in.length - 1].max);
    this.in.push({ val: x, max: newMax });
    this.total += x;
  }
  pop() {
    if (this.out.length === 0) {
      while (this.in.length > 0) {
        const v = this.in.pop().val;
        const newMax = this.out.length === 0 ? v : Math.max(v, this.out[this.out.length - 1].max);
        this.out.push({ val: v, max: newMax });
      }
    }
    if (this.out.length === 0) return undefined;
    const popped = this.out.pop();
    this.total -= popped.val;
    return popped.val;
  }
  max() {
    const maxIn = this.in.length === 0 ? -Infinity : this.in[this.in.length - 1].max;
    const maxOut = this.out.length === 0 ? -Infinity : this.out[this.out.length - 1].max;
    if (maxIn === -Infinity && maxOut === -Infinity) return undefined;
    return Math.max(maxIn, maxOut);
  }
  sum() {
    return this.total;
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support a sliding window of last N elements for max and sum?
- How would you extend to also provide a min() with the same complexity guarantees?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates practical data-structure composition: a queue with constant-time min tracking, a common interview pattern for streams and real-time dashboards.

## Key Concepts

- Two-stack queue pattern
- Per-stack min tracking
- Amortized analysis and edge-case handling for duplicates

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinQueue {
  constructor(){ this.inStack = []; this.outStack = []; }
  enqueue(x){ const minIn = this.inStack.length ? Math.min(x, this.inStack[this.inStack.length-1][1]) : x; this.inStack.push([x, minIn]); }
  dequeue(){ if (!this.outStack.length){ while (this.inStack.length){ const [val] = this.inStack.pop(); const minOut = this.outStack.length ? Math.min(val, this.outStack[this.outStack.length-1][1]) : val; this.outStack.push([val, minOut]); } }
    const popped = this.outStack.pop(); return popped ? popped[0] : undefined; }
  getMin(){ const minIn = this.inStack.length ? this.inStack[this.inStack.length-1][1] : Infinity; const minOut = this.outStack.length ? this.outStack[this.outStack.length-1][1] : Infinity; return Math.min(minIn, minOut); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for a circular buffer with fixed capacity? 
- Compare this with a min-heap alternative and discuss trade-offs.

